<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Francis Burton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Richard Burton</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Richard Francis Burton was a British explorer, soldier, linguist and writer. In 1855 he became the first European known to enter the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>, and in 1857–1858 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a> explored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. He had made a disguised pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 1853, and he later translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-thousand-and-one-nights">Arabian Nights</a> and the Kama Sutra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton.jpg" alt="Richard Francis Burton" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rischgitz/Stringer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_Francis_Burton_by_Rischgitz,_1864.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Richard Burton was born in Torquay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, on 19 March 1821, although his autobiography incorrectly places his birth at Barham House in Elstree, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>. His father was an Anglo-Irish army officer, and his mother, Martha Baker, was the daughter of a wealthy Hertfordshire squire, Richard Baker, after whom he was named. He grew up between Elstree and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where his father sought a better climate for his asthma. He abandoned his university studies and in 1842 was commissioned into the Bombay Army, beginning an eighteen-year military career.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> he served in the 18th Regiment of Native Infantry under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-james-napier">Charles James Napier</a> and earned the nickname Ruffian Dick. He passed six formal language examinations and became proficient in Persian, Arabic, Hindustani, Gujarati, Punjabi, Sindhi, Saraiki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>. In 1853 he made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in disguise, as a Persian mirza and then a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> sheikh, doctor and dervish, having prepared by studying Muslim culture.</p><p>In 1854-1855 he led a Royal Geographical Society expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a>. Disguised as an Arab merchant, he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> on 3 January 1855 and spent ten days there, officially a guest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> but in reality his prisoner. In 1857 he and Speke left Zanzibar for the African Great Lakes with a caravan of 132 people, hindered by malaria and other fevers, and became the first Europeans known to have seen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. Burton married Isabel Arundel on 22 January 1861 and then entered the Diplomatic Service as consul at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Fernando Po</a>. He later served at Santos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, and was knighted in 1886.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Burton wrote over forty books, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a> and the Cameroons Mountains (1863), The Highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> (1869) and Letters from the Battlefields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> (1870). With Forster Fitzgerald Arbuthnot he created the Kama Shastra Society to circulate privately books that would have been illegal to publish after the Obscene Publications Act 1857. It printed his translation of The Book of the Thousand Nights and a Night in ten volumes in 1885, in a subscribers-only edition of one thousand. Burton died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> on 20 October 1890 of a heart attack. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-princess-imperial-of-brazil">Isabel</a> afterwards burned many of his papers, including a planned translation of The Perfumed Garden, an act that remains controversial, though much of his writing survives at the Huntington Library.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard Francis Burton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zanzibar was also the starting point for European exploration of the interior: <b>Richard Burton</b>, John Hanning Speke, David Livingstone and Henry Morton Stanley all outfitted their expeditions in the town, often with the help of its merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Since 1982 the International Astronomical Union has named features on Saturn's moon Enceladus after characters and places in <b>Richard Francis Burton</b>'s translation of the Nights.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Richard Burton</b>, the first European known to enter, visited in 1855.</blockquote><a href="/state/harar-emirate">Harar Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>Explorers including <b>Burton</b>, Speke, Livingstone and Stanley stayed at Tabora.</blockquote><a href="/state/unyanyembe">Unyanyembe</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Torquay, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.46,-3.53&amp;year=1865" aria-label="Torquay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4469 411 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Richard Francis Burton"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4919 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4919 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.46,-3.53&amp;year=1865">What was here in 1865? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
